\f1\fs26 Instead of being executed, Emil Kalheri decided he\'92d go to the pub.\
It wasn\'92t that he\'92d developed an intention to squib on the final obligations of the contract\'97a 
\f2\i Blank Contract
\f1\i0 , Special Agent Ajent Jones called it. He\'92d already performed the grisly task of mincing a hole in a man\'92s chest with a shotgun, and taken the most perfunctory steps to make it look a suicide. And he\'92d acquired the strange item Agent Jones said the man had stolen.\
It went down exactly as Agent Jones said it would. Emil arrived at the office tower shortly after midnight and strode at the 2-storey glass doors as if he owned the place. To Emil\'92s impish glee, the office security system 
\f2\i did
\f1\i0  recognise the brand and quality of the midnight blue suit he was wearing (supplied by Agent Jones) and granted him, the wearer, an all-access pass. In the lobby, doors opened on a luxurious Airborne-only elevator which exalted him more the two hundred floors into the air, and admitted him to an office suite dimly aglow with the navigation gear of passing hover-copts and lighting from other towers. Emil marvelled how clear the night air was above the Layer. He stopped at reception\'92s huge plate windows, transfixed for many seconds, as he tried to make sense of a golden, milky light high in the sky. \'93You, idiot, Emil,\'94 he told himself. \'93That\'92s the moon.\'94 He chuckled at the marvels the Airborne enjoyed as a matter of course.\
Finding Travis Teh\'92s office was simple. As Agent Jones had predicted, Teh was the only one working this late, and his office light was on. When threatened with the shotgun, Teh was all guppy lips and jelly limbs, and eagerly handed over the item with shaky fingers. As per instructions, Emil shot him anyway, with the barrels of the gun shoved between Teh\'92s jaw and Adam\'92s apple. Teh even obliged Emil\'92s request to hold the gun\'92s grip as Emil\'92s finger caressed the trigger. Perhaps Teh thought it an opportunity to yank the gun away before Emil could fire. He was wrong.\
Emil felt powerful. So powerful, he experienced an overwhelming urge to pee. He found the office\'92s opulent bathrooms, stood at the urinal and caressed himself for longer than functionally required. He felt so alive, even the pain of his testicular cancer was tamed.\
\'93Tax you!\'94 Emil cursed his balls. \'93You won\'92t get the chance to kill me!\'94\
No, an unknown assassin would have that honour, once Emil delivered the item. Agent Jones promised it would be quick and clean. Emil cared little; any death would be quicker and cleaner than his own gonads eating him from the inside out. \
Emil triumphantly zipped the fly of his immaculate suit pants, and resolved against washing his hands.\
