\f1\fs26 Emil found a 24-hour bar a couple of blocks from the office tower. With the kind of satisfaction only a gambler could muster after a big win, Emil strode for a seat at the bar. He unbuttoned his coat with a flourish as he sat down.\
\'93You sure, you\'92re in the right place, partner?\'94 the bartender asked, eyeing Emil\'92s outfit.\
\'93I\'92m exactly where I want to be,\'94 Emile answered, and lifted his arm so the bartender could scan his armpit for his consumer account. \'93Your best tequila, barkeep, and keep them flowing.\'94\
The bartender squinted at the bar\'92s Grogify tablet, reading. \'93Looks like your account runs out before \'85 half a bottle.\'94\
\'93Fit for purpose, my good soul,\'94 Emil said. He\'92d drink his account dry, pickled-happy the contract he signed guaranteed his family would be provided for, for the rest of their lives. A momentary wave of sorrow took him. He missed his wife Peggy, and his kids, Becky and Roger. They\'92d be so much better off without him.\
Emil downed the first shot of gold liquid and pushed the glass for replenishing. He grunted, remembered the item he still had to deliver to Special Agent Ajent Jones, and fished it from his breast pocket. He screwed his face into a pinch of unanswered questions. The disc, twice the width of his thumb, gave no clue it was worth killing for. It wasn\'92t even metal, as far as Emil could tell. It was dull and tough and bendable, although it sprang back to shape when he stopped squeezing it. He though it might be crocodile hide, though he\'92d never seen a crocodile, nor the genuine hide of any animal, for that matter.\
Then it glinted. Or Emil thought it glinted. It could be the lighting in the bar, playing tricks. He downed another shot and waited for it to glint again. When it inertly refused, he bounced it in his palm, testing its weight. Then he tossed it up, like tossing a coin.\
The stool under him trembled. The tide of his tequila splashed. Lights over the bar swayed. Glasses trembled and shook. Bottles leapt from shelves. Customers squealed.\
Emil clasped the disc in his hand.\
\'93What the tax was that?\'94 the bartender blurted. \'93Earthquake? In Ulyssia?\'94\
\'93I dunno,\'94 Emil said. \'93I just thought how awesome this tequila is.\'94 He drained the shot and slid the glass to the bartender for a refill. Emil was lucky; the tequila bottle was still intact.\
